title: Destroyed, and checked rather than assumed
subtitle: week-22-container-image-security - the teardown verification, run after terraform destroy
width: 1050
---
$ python scripts/hcp_destroy.py week-22-dev

  week-22-container-image-security: 13 slot(s) declared, 11 captured
  every declared figure is captured or retired -- safe to destroy.   [ok]
  week-22-dev: 26 resources -> destroy run run-UcvQxgEL4N2Ym11T

  to destroy: 24
  FINAL: applied                                                      [ok]

$ bash scripts/cleanup.sh

  == 1. Registry scanning configuration ==
     scanType: BASIC                                                  [ok]

  == 2. Amazon Inspector (separate service, separate bill) ==
     ec2:             DISABLED                                        [ok]
     ecr:             DISABLED                                        [ok]
     lambda:          DISABLED                                        [ok]
     lambdaCode:      DISABLED                                        [ok]
     codeRepository:  DISABLED                                        [ok]

  == 3. ECR repositories ==
     none remaining                                                   [ok]

  == 4. Signer signing profiles ==
     none active                                                      [ok]

  == 5. Signing configuration ==
     none                                                             [ok]

  == 6. Log groups ==
     none remaining                                                   [ok]

Note what is NOT printed: no "still ENHANCED -- resetting" and no "still
ENABLED -- disabling". terraform destroy had already removed both, because
aws_inspector2_enabler and the scanning configuration are Terraform resources
in this build.

The script checks anyway. In an earlier week a CloudWatch anomaly detector
survived a destroy and kept billing for days, because nothing in the state
file owned it. Account-level settings are exactly where "the destroy probably
handled it" should be turned into a command that looks.
